(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Formulate the fundamental definition of an engineering truss, its four classical analytical assumptions, and the internal static determinacy criterion.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structural Context & Equilibrium Classification:) Tj
T*
(   This analysis addresses internal member forces and structural determinacy for plane truss) Tj
T*
(   systems.) Tj
T*
(   Governing criteria: All joints are frictionless spherical pins; loads act exclusively at nodes;) Tj
T*
(   members are ideal two-force axial elements carrying pure tension \(+T\) or compression \(-C\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Derivation & Step-by-Step Joint/Section Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   Theory of Engineering Trusses and Determinacy Criteria:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition:) Tj
T*
(   A truss is an engineering structural framework formed by interconnecting slender structural) Tj
T*
(   members at their terminal ends in triangulated patterns to span long spans efficiently while) Tj
T*
(   resisting external loads purely through axial forces.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Four Fundamental Idealizations in Classical Truss Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   - Idealization 1: All structural members are connected at their joints by frictionless,) Tj
T*
(   frictionless cylindrical pins / hinges.) Tj
T*
(   - Idealization 2: The longitudinal centroidal axes of all members meeting at any joint) Tj
T*
(   intersect at a single concurrent mathematical point.) Tj
T*
(   - Idealization 3: All external active loads \(dead loads, live loads, wind forces\) and support) Tj
T*
(   reactions are applied strictly at the pin joints \(nodes\).) Tj
T*
(   - Idealization 4: The self-weight of individual members is either negligible compared to) Tj
T*
(   applied external loads or is apportioned as half-weights to the two connecting end joints.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Resulting Mechanical Behavior:) Tj
T*
(   Under these four assumptions, every constituent member behaves strictly as a Two-Force Member,) Tj
T*
(   developing purely axial tension \(T\) or axial compression \(C\) with zero bending moments and zero) Tj
T*
(   transverse shear forces.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Internal Static Determinacy Criterion in 2D Planar Trusses:) Tj
T*
(   Let m = total number of members, j = total number of pin joints.) Tj
T*
(   At each joint, exactly 2 independent equations of static equilibrium are available \(Sigma Fx =) Tj
T*
(   0, Sigma Fy = 0\).) Tj
T*
(   Total available joint equilibrium equations = 2 * j.) Tj
T*
(   Subtracting 3 external reaction equations for basic determinacy yields the criterion:) Tj
T*
(   - Perfect \(Statically Determinate\) Truss: m = 2 * j - 3. The truss is rigid and all member) Tj
T*
(   forces can be solved uniquely by statics alone.) Tj
T*
(   - Deficient \(Hypostatic / Unstable Mechanism\): m < 2 * j - 3. Lacks sufficient members to) Tj
T*
(   maintain stable geometry under general loads.) Tj
T*
(   - Redundant \(Statically Indeterminate\): m > 2 * j - 3. Possesses redundant internal members;) Tj
T*
(   requires deformation compatibility \(elastic strain energy\) to solve.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Structural Equilibrium & Kinematic Compatibility Check:) Tj
T*
(   - External determinacy verified via global equilibrium equations: Sigma Fx = 0, Sigma Fy = 0,) Tj
T*
(   Sigma M = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Internal determinacy satisfied via Maxwell's criterion: m = 2*j - 3.) Tj
T*
(   - Joint node equilibrium confirmed: Vector resultant of all concurrent member forces vanishes) Tj
T*
(   at each pin.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
