(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Compare the Method of Joints and Method of Sections in detail, detailing their analytical procedures and relative advantages.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structural Context & Equilibrium Classification:) Tj
T*
(   This analysis addresses internal member forces and structural determinacy for plane truss) Tj
T*
(   systems.) Tj
T*
(   Governing criteria: All joints are frictionless spherical pins; loads act exclusively at nodes;) Tj
T*
(   members are ideal two-force axial elements carrying pure tension \(+T\) or compression \(-C\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Derivation & Step-by-Step Joint/Section Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   Comparative Engineering Analysis: Method of Joints vs Method of Sections:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Method of Joints:) Tj
T*
(   - Core Principle: Focuses on the equilibrium of individual pin joints isolated as particles.) Tj
T*
(   - Governing Equations: At each joint: Sigma Fx = 0 and Sigma Fy = 0 \(2 scalar equations\).) Tj
T*
(   - Procedure:) Tj
T*
(   * Step 1: Calculate global support reactions from overall equilibrium \(Sigma Fx = 0, Sigma Fy =) Tj
T*
(   0, Sigma M = 0\).) Tj
T*
(   * Step 2: Identify a starting joint having at least one known force and no more than two) Tj
T*
(   unknown member forces.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 3: Draw the FBD of the joint, assuming unknown forces are tensile \(pulling away from) Tj
T*
(   joint\).) Tj
T*
(   * Step 4: Solve the 2 simultaneous linear equations. A positive sign indicates tension;) Tj
T*
(   negative indicates compression.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 5: Proceed systematically through adjacent joints until all members are analyzed.) Tj
T*
(   - Primary Advantage: Provides a complete, comprehensive solution for every member force in the) Tj
T*
(   entire truss.) Tj
T*
(   - Limitation: Error-accumulating; computationally tedious if only a few specific internal) Tj
T*
(   members are required.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Method of Sections:) Tj
T*
(   - Core Principle: Cuts the truss into two completely separated rigid bodies by an imaginary) Tj
T*
(   section line passing through the target members.) Tj
T*
(   - Governing Equations: For either isolated section: Sigma Fx = 0, Sigma Fy = 0, and Sigma M_O =) Tj
T*
(   0 \(3 independent scalar equations\).) Tj
T*
(   - Procedure:) Tj
T*
(   * Step 1: Compute external support reactions.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 2: Cut an imaginary section passing through a maximum of three unknown members whose) Tj
T*
(   forces are desired.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 3: Choose the simpler of the two isolated halves and draw its comprehensive FBD.) Tj
T*
(   * Step 4: Apply moment equilibrium \(Sigma M = 0\) about the point of intersection of two unknown) Tj
T*
(   members to directly solve for the third member.) Tj
T*
(   - Primary Advantage: Fast, direct determination of specific internal member forces \(e.g. at) Tj
T*
(   midspan\) in a single calculation step.) Tj
T*
ET

