BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the structural behavior and member force distributions in Pratt vs Howe bridge trusses under moving traffic loads.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structural Context & Equilibrium Classification:) Tj
T*
(   This analysis addresses internal member forces and structural determinacy for plane truss) Tj
T*
(   systems.) Tj
T*
(   Governing criteria: All joints are frictionless spherical pins; loads act exclusively at nodes;) Tj
T*
(   members are ideal two-force axial elements carrying pure tension \(+T\) or compression \(-C\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Derivation & Step-by-Step Joint/Section Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   Comparative Analysis: Pratt vs Howe Bridge Trusses:) Tj
T*
(   1. Pratt Truss Configuration:) Tj
T*
(   - Web Geometry: Vertical members are in compression; diagonal web members slope downward) Tj
T*
(   towards the center of the span.) Tj
T*
(   - Behavior under Gravity Loads:) Tj
T*
(   * As traffic dead and live loads push downward, the diagonals are stretched in AXIAL TENSION.) Tj
T*
(   * The vertical struts are loaded in AXIAL COMPRESSION.) Tj
T*
(   - Structural Advantage in Steel Design:) Tj
T*
(   Because slender steel members are highly susceptible to Euler buckling under compression \(P_cr) Tj
T*
(   proportional to 1/L^2\), having longer diagonal members in tension and shorter vertical members) Tj
T*
(   in compression yields a lighter, more economical steel truss.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Howe Truss Configuration:) Tj
T*
(   - Web Geometry: Diagonals slope upward towards the center of the span; vertical members act as) Tj
T*
(   vertical ties.) Tj
T*
(   - Behavior under Gravity Loads:) Tj
T*
(   * Diagonal members are pushed into AXIAL COMPRESSION.) Tj
T*
(   * Vertical members are stretched in AXIAL TENSION.) Tj
T*
(   - Historical Context in Timber Construction:) Tj
T*
ET

